  "habitat": "Pairs in summer and small flocks in winter, in various wooded habitats and in many towns. It frequents large trees and feeds on the ground.",
  "summary": "A common resident. Grey head, chestnut upperparts and a single white wing-bar. The bill is black when breeding and horn-coloured otherwise. Sexes are alike. The immature has a streaked mantle. The call is a repetitive cheep-chirp, the first note descending and the second ascending, plus an occasional trill. The Kemp paper name Greyheaded Sparrow and the code RB were left unchanged.",
